WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.620
Robots are steadily improving at many tasks,

00:00:02.620 --> 00:00:06.520
and even outdoing humans at some of them.

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:08.640
But they’re still incredibly clumsy when

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.680
it comes to fine motor movements in small

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:12.809
small joints, like our hands.

00:00:12.809 --> 00:00:15.100
Typically, even toddlers can blow the most

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:17.440
cutting edge robots out of the water when

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:19.380
it comes to incorporating feedback into how

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.510
we touch, move or grip things.

00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:25.200
For example, when you get into a new car with

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.500
a gas pedal more sensitive than your old one,

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:30.220
you adjust your foot pressure accordingly.

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:32.380
Your brain, your body, and the environment

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:34.550
work together to help you determine the right

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:37.070
movement and execute it.

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:39.460
Researchers wanted to build robots capable

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:41.739
of using this three-pronged approach – mind,

00:00:41.739 --> 00:00:45.240
body, environment - to execute

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:47.850
more complex and sensitive movements.

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:50.960
So they used 3D printing to create a skeleton-like

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:53.600
hand, integrating materials with different

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:56.500
flexibilities to simulate the complex arrangement

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:00.060
of bones, joints, and ligaments in the human hand.

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:02.580
They built several hands with different levels

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:04.340
of stiffness in each joint to see what kind

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:07.029
of behaviors they could achieve.

00:01:07.029 --> 00:01:08.980
To test the ability of the skeleton hand to

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:11.820
perform challenging mechanical tasks, they

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:14.280
had it play piano music in different styles,

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:17.090
something that can trip up even the most dexterous

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:19.020
of humans.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:20.900
The hand was able to play the piano using

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:23.679
the staccato technique with slightly shortened,

00:01:23.679 --> 00:01:26.400
bouncy notes.

00:01:36.439 --> 00:01:39.300
It could also play glissando, where you slide

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:41.180
smoothly between the notes

00:01:44.349 --> 00:01:46.400
The researchers analyzed how much force the

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:48.719
robot was using and how quickly it was hitting

00:01:48.719 --> 00:01:51.680
each note, and they found a strong match to the

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:53.829
force and timing of a human playing the same

00:01:53.829 --> 00:01:55.440
pieces.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.920
While the skeleton hand did not capture all

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:00.580
of the complexities of the human hand, the researchers

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:02.700
see it as a step forward in creating robots

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:05.759
capable of more complex, nuanced interactions

00:02:05.759 --> 00:02:08.740
with objects in their environment, by taking

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:10.530
advantage of the natural flexibility of the

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.069
human hand.

00:02:12.069 --> 00:02:13.900
The hands could be one day be used for a variety

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:17.099
of job that require a delicate touch like

00:02:17.099 --> 00:02:19.640
picking fruit off of trees or feeling for

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:21.650
tumors or unusual tissues.

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:24.420
But for now, they’ll stick to tickling the

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:26.680
ivories, and they might even have a festive

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:29.540
song or two up their sleeves.

